WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.893
Writing Learning Objectives.

2
00:00:02.893 --> 00:00:08.368
ID 553 - Module 4: Objectives and Aligned Assessment.

3
00:00:08.368 --> 00:00:14.567
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:24.126
A learning objective states what the learner will be able to
do after instruction, under what conditions, and to what
standard.

5
00:00:24.126 --> 00:00:27.018
It's written for three audiences at once.

6
00:00:27.018 --> 00:00:30.334
It tells the designer what to teach and assess.

7
00:00:30.334 --> 00:00:33.508
It tells the learner what success looks like.

8
00:00:33.508 --> 00:00:36.542
And it tells the evaluator what to measure.

9
00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:40.421
An objective that can't serve all three isn't finished.

10
00:00:40.421 --> 00:00:49.521
Objectives come straight from the task analysis: each
numbered element above the entry-skill line becomes one or
more objectives.

11
00:00:49.521 --> 00:00:53.895
That derivation is what keeps instruction tied to
performance.

12
00:00:54.495 --> 00:00:57.584
Two formats carry the same information.

13
00:00:57.584 --> 00:01:11.922
Mager's three components are performance, stated with an
observable verb; conditions, the circumstances under which
the performance happens; and criterion, how well it must be
done.

14
00:01:11.922 --> 00:01:18.576
The ABCD format adds the audience explicitly: audience,
behavior, condition, degree.

15
00:01:18.576 --> 00:01:20.319
Here's a complete one.

16
00:01:20.319 --> 00:01:40.914
Given a patient wristband, a medication order in the records
system, and a handheld scanner, the nurse will verify
patient identity and medication match with one hundred
percent accuracy across ten simulated administrations,
including two with mismatch alerts.

17
00:01:40.914 --> 00:01:44.716
Conditions, performance, criterion, all present.

18
00:01:45.316 --> 00:01:47.328
The verb is the objective.

19
00:01:47.328 --> 00:01:54.677
Understand, know, appreciate, and be familiar with describe
internal states nobody can observe.

20
00:01:54.677 --> 00:02:05.431
Replace them with what the learner would do to show the
state: list, describe, classify, calculate, demonstrate,
diagnose, design, justify.

21
00:02:05.431 --> 00:02:11.620
Bloom's revised taxonomy organizes cognitive verbs from
remember through create.

22
00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:16.649
Use it as a vocabulary, not as a ladder to climb in every
lesson.

23
00:02:16.649 --> 00:02:22.684
If the job requires applying a procedure, an apply-level
objective is correct.

24
00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:30.033
Adding create-level objectives to look rigorous misaligns
the instruction with the performance.

25
00:02:30.633 --> 00:02:33.163
Let's rewrite five bad objectives.

26
00:02:33.163 --> 00:02:37.106
Number one: students will understand the ADDIE model.

27
00:02:37.106 --> 00:02:41.793
The verb is unobservable and there's no condition or
criterion.

28
00:02:41.793 --> 00:02:55.036
Rewritten: given a description of an instructional design
project, students will identify which ADDIE phase each
described activity belongs to, with at least nine of ten
correct.

29
00:02:55.036 --> 00:02:59.649
Number two: learners will be familiar with the records
system.

30
00:02:59.649 --> 00:03:02.179
That's a topic, not a performance.

31
00:03:02.179 --> 00:03:13.116
Rewritten: using the practice environment, learners will
locate a named patient and open the medication
administration record within sixty seconds.

32
00:03:13.716 --> 00:03:17.893
Number three: participants will learn about accessibility.

33
00:03:17.893 --> 00:03:23.800
The verb describes what the instruction does, not what the
learner does afterward.

34
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:33.452
Rewritten: given a short e-learning module, participants
will identify all accessibility violations from a list of
eight common types.

35
00:03:33.452 --> 00:03:36.909
Number four: nurses will administer medications.

36
00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:48.650
It's missing conditions and a criterion, and it's far too
broad; it needs to be split into a terminal objective and
the enabling objectives from the task analysis.

37
00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.963
Number five: students will write a good essay.

38
00:03:51.963 --> 00:03:53.836
'Good' is not a criterion.

39
00:03:53.836 --> 00:04:04.856
Rewritten: students will write a five-hundred-word
persuasive essay that meets all four criteria on the
attached rubric at the proficient level or above.

40
00:04:05.456 --> 00:04:11.162
The terminal objective describes the goal performance;
there's usually one per unit.

41
00:04:11.162 --> 00:04:20.469
Enabling objectives describe the subordinate skills that
build to it, and your task analysis hierarchy gives you that
structure for free.

42
00:04:20.469 --> 00:04:25.020
Number every objective with the task analysis element it
came from.

43
00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:32.016
And share the terminal objective and the assessment criteria
with learners at the start of instruction.

44
00:04:32.016 --> 00:04:37.858
Well-written objectives make relevance visible and give
learners a standard to aim at.

45
00:04:37.858 --> 00:04:40.100
Hiding the standard helps nobody.

46
00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:47.640
For the objective clinic, write your terminal objective and
two enabling objectives and post them for critique.

47
00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.308
Expect to rewrite them; that's what the clinic is for.
